<h1 style="font-family:'Source Serif 4',Georgia,serif;font-weight:800;font-size:clamp(30px,7.2vw,42px);line-height:1.12;color:#1F2419">7 Signs Your Body Is Holding On to Stress, <span class="hl">Not Just Calories</span></h1>
<p class="t" style="font-size:20px">You eat clean, keep moving, try everything, and still wake up puffy. If the last few years asked a lot of you (a divorce, a betrayal, caring for everyone but yourself), your body may still be carrying it. Count how many of these sound like you.</p>
<p style="font-size:13px;color:#5E6656">By the Resilia editorial team · Updated November 2026 · 3 min read</p>
<img src="{{ 't06a-hero.jpg' | asset_url }}" alt="A tired woman leaning on her kitchen counter in the evening" style="width:100%;aspect-ratio:4/3;object-fit:cover;border-radius:14px">
<section class="sec" style="border-top:0;padding-top:0">
<div class="sign"><div class="num">1</div><div><h3>Puffy face first thing in the morning</h3><p>Stress keeps your body on alert, and overnight it holds on instead of letting go.</p></div></div>
<div class="sign"><div class="num">2</div><div><h3>Your jawline isn&#x27;t where it used to be</h3><p>When the body holds on, the face is often where it shows first.</p></div></div>
<div class="sign"><div class="num">3</div><div><h3>Your belly feels hard and swollen by afternoon</h3><p>A body running on stress tends to hold on around the middle.</p></div></div>
<div class="sign"><div class="num">4</div><div><h3>You&#x27;re done for the day by 6pm</h3><p>Years of running on stress empty the tank by evening.</p></div></div>
<div class="sign"><div class="num">5</div><div><h3>What used to work doesn&#x27;t move anything</h3><p>Less food and more cardio read as more stress, so the body holds on tighter.</p></div></div>
<div class="sign"><div class="num">6</div><div><h3>You wake at 3am and can&#x27;t switch off</h3><p>Stress that never switches off follows you to bed.</p></div></div>
<div class="sign"><div class="num">7</div><div><h3>There&#x27;s a dress you&#x27;ve stopped trying on</h3><p>It&#x27;s not discipline you&#x27;re missing.</p></div></div>
<div class="box" style="text-align:center"><p style="font-size:19px;font-weight:800">3 or more?</p><p class="t" style="font-size:17px">You’re not imagining it, and you’re not failing. Here’s what’s likely going on.</p></div>
